# Terminal

Neovim has a built-in terminal. This config adds a single shell that slides
up from the bottom, and easy ways to treat its output like a normal buffer:
search it, move around it and yank from it without the mouse.

    `<C-/>`{normal}          open / hide the terminal (works from inside it too)
    `<Esc><Esc>`{normal}     leave typing mode: now the output is a normal buffer
    `<C-q>`{normal}          same thing; also works in the Claude panel
    `i`{normal} or `a`{normal}       start typing in the shell again

Hiding the terminal keeps the shell running, with its history and current
directory. Typing `exit` closes it for good; the next `<C-/>`{normal} starts a new one.

# Lesson 4.1: Toggle

 1. Press `<C-/>`{normal}. A shell opens at the bottom, ready for typing.
 2. Run `pwd`. Press `<C-/>`{normal} to hide it, then `<C-/>`{normal} again. `pwd`'s output is
    still there.

The shell starts in the working directory of the tab where you first open it.
Opened from the playground tab, that is the playground.

# Lesson 4.2: Scrollback

Once you leave typing mode, every vim motion works on the output: `gg`{normal}, `G`{normal},
`/`{normal} search, `<C-u>`{normal} / `<C-d>`{normal}, visual mode, `y`{normal}. The window keeps the last
100,000 lines.

 1. Open the terminal and run:

        seq 1 5000 && echo "tutor-$((6 * 7))"

 2. Press `<Esc><Esc>`{normal}. Press `gg`{normal} to go to the top of the output, then
    search with `/4999`{normal}.
 3. Press `G`{normal}, move to the `tutor-...` line and yank the word with `yiW`{normal}.
 4. Come back to this lesson (hide the terminal with `<C-/>`{normal}, or `<C-k>`{normal}),
    put the cursor on the `____` below and type `viWp`{normal} to replace it with
    what you yanked.

    terminal output: ____

 5. Open the terminal again. It is still in normal mode, so press `i`{normal} to type.

# Lesson 4.3: Moving between windows

In typing mode, keys go to the shell, so window commands don't work. Leave
typing mode first, then move as usual:

    `<Esc><Esc>`{normal} `<C-k>`{normal}     terminal → the window above
    `<C-j>`{normal}             back down to the terminal

For a second, separate terminal, use plain Neovim: `:vsplit | terminal`{vim} or
`:tab terminal`{vim}. They get the same scrollback and `<Esc><Esc>`{normal}.

# Lesson 4.4: Gotchas

- `<Esc><Esc>`{normal} needs both presses within a second. A single `<Esc>`{normal} still reaches
  the shell, but only after that pause.
- In the Claude panel, `<Esc><Esc>`{normal} belongs to Claude (it edits your previous
  message). Use `<C-q>`{normal} there.
- Many terminals send `<C-/>`{normal} as `<C-_>`{normal}. Both are mapped.

